{"content":{"blocks":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01AjrnJWgAXa3TddUJCVpumB","input":{"content":"# Product intent\n\n**Status:** design-stage impressions. Nothing here is committed; it exists to\nframe the problem and record early opinions, and is expected to shift.\n\n## Purpose\n\nContext Graph makes the relationships *among a repository's artifacts* explicit\nand addressable. Code, docs, conversations, commits, and decisions are all\npartial expressions of the same work, densely connected — a file imports\nanother, a doc explains a module, a conversation settles why a line exists, a\ncommit implements that decision — but those connections are never recorded as\nfirst-class structure. This tool derives them into one typed graph that both a\nhuman and a machine can traverse.\n\nConversations are the first artifact type wired up, because Conversation Ledger\nalready stores them with enough provenance to reconstruct each one and\nturnbridge already writes `continuation` lineage edges into it. Code and doc\nnodes follow. The graph is heterogeneous from the start so those later nodes\njoin the same structure rather than forcing a rebuild.\n\n## Behavioral commitments (intended)\n\n- Derive edges from evidence that already exists (ledger events, import\n  statements, doc links, commit metadata); never invent a relationship the\n  artifacts do not support.\n- Keep every edge traceable back to what justifies it — a graph is a derived\n  view, and the underlying artifacts stay authoritative.\n- Treat the graph as rebuildable: it is an index over the repository and its\n  ledger, not a second source of truth; re-deriving from the same inputs yields\n  the same graph.\n- Distinguish edge *kinds* (continuation, supersession, imports, documents,\n  discusses, implements, shared-file, same-author, temporal) rather than\n  flattening everything into one \"related\" link.\n- Make edge confidence and its basis visible, so a weak co-occurrence edge is\n  not mistaken for an explicit reference.\n\n## Non-goals (for now)\n\n- Deciding which artifact is \"correct\" or ranking intent — that is\n  intent-recall's job; this tool supplies structure, not judgment.\n- Capturing conversations or moving them between CLIs — that is turnbridge.\n- Becoming a second store: nodes reference artifacts and hold derived edges,\n  not copies of their content.\n\n## Relationship to the other tools\n\nContext Graph is a **reader** of Conversation Ledger (and, over time, of the\nworking tree and git history), a peer of intent-recall (both derive views from\nthe ledger; neither depends on the other), and a **producer** for downstream\nconsumers — most concretely\n[tagseq2tagseq](https://github.com/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq), whose training\npipeline traverses a text-attributed document graph. turnbridge already emits\n`continuation` lineage edges into the ledger; this tool is the first that reads\nthem as graph structure rather than as per-row labels.\n\n## Scope and sequencing\n\nThe end state is one graph spanning every cross-file connection in a repo. The\nsequence to get there — conversations first (the ledger exists), then code and\ndoc extractors, then commit/decision links — is a build order, not a set of\nseparate products. Whether every stage lives in this repo or some split into a\nlater sibling is undecided; the design assumes a single shared graph either way,\nwhich is why node identity and edge shape are kept uniform now.\n","file_path":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/context-graph/docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md"},"name":"Write","type":"tool_use"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"49da723e6046dbccfcf0e3966f6e68a44a5964dd","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"id":"ev2-a8ff9f84ebda47204a1c8952fc068a5f1a8e7ddf5fe1efc83ddf4d4a997b5669","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-4-8","type":"agent"},"session_id":"c158a677-efd7-4ff4-87ac-1