le structure. Context\nGraph makes them explicit — typed edges, each traceable to the evidence that\njustifies it.\n\nThe first artifacts it wires up are **conversations**, because\n[Conversation Ledger](https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger)\nalready stores them durably and turnbridge already writes lineage edges into\nit. Code and doc nodes follow, sharing one identifier namespace so a\nconversation and the file it discusses are joinable.\n\nThis repository is intentionally at the design stage. Nothing here is locked\nin — it records current impressions and the shape of the problem, not a\ncommitted API. See [product intent](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) and the\n[WIP technical design](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md).\n\n## Where it sits\n\n- **conversation-ledger** — neutral, immutable store of conversation events;\n  the first source Context Graph reads.\n- **turnbridge** — captures/moves conversations between CLIs; already writes\n  `continuation` lineage edges into the ledger for this tool to surface.\n- **intent-recall** — reads the ledger to answer \"what was intended?\"; a\n  sibling consumer, not a dependency.\n- **context-graph** (this repo) — derives the explicit relationship graph over\n  the ledger now, and over code/docs as extractors are added.\n- **[tagseq2tagseq](https://github.com/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq)** — trains\n  language models by traversing a text-attributed document graph; the natural\n  downstream consumer of the graph this tool emits (see the design doc).\n\n## License\n\n[MIT](LICENSE)\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/context-graph/README.md","originalFile":"# Conversation Graph\n\nDerive a navigable graph of how conversations connect — to each other and, in\ntime, to the code and docs they discuss.\n\nConversation Graph reads the durable record in\n[Conversation Ledger](https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger) and\nturns implicit relationships between conversations (a conversation bridged into\nanother CLI, one that supersedes an earlier decision, several that touch the\nsame files) into explicit, addressable edges. Its first consumer is a human\nasking \"how does this discussion relate to that one?\"; its second is a training\npipeline that traverses the graph.\n\nThis repository is intentionally at the design stage. Nothing here is locked\nin — it records current impressions and the shape of the problem, not a\ncommitted API. See [product intent](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) and the\n[WIP technical design](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md).\n\n## Where it sits\n\n- **conversation-ledger** — neutral, immutable store of conversation events\n  (the source of truth this tool reads).\n- **turnbridge** — captures/moves conversations between CLIs; already writes\n  `continuation` lineage edges into the ledger that this tool would surface.\n- **intent-recall** — reads the ledger to answer \"what was intended?\"; a\n  sibling consumer, not a dependency.\n- **conversation-graph** (this repo) — reads the ledger, emits an explicit\n  relationship graph.\n- **[tagseq2tagseq](https://github.com/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq)** — trains\n  language models by traversing a text-attributed document graph; a natural\n  downstream consumer of the graph this tool emits (see the design doc).\n\n## License\n\n[MIT](LICENSE)\n","structuredPatch":[{"lines":["-# Conversation Graph","+# Context Graph"," ","-Derive a navigable graph of how conversations connect — to each other and, in","-time, to the code and docs they discuss.","+A navigable graph of how everything in a repository connects — code, docs, and","+conversations, as one heterogeneous graph."," ","-Conversation Graph reads the durable record in","-[Conversation Ledger](https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger) and","-turns implicit relationships between conversations (a conversation bridged into","-another CLI, one that supersedes an earlier decision, several that touch the","-same files) into explicit, addressable edges. Its