mit`, `decision` follow), a\nstable `normalized_identifier` in one shared namespace, and a reference to the\nunderlying artifact rather than a copy of it. Keeping one namespace is the whole\npoint: a `conversation` node and the `file` node it discusses must be joinable\nwithout a later rebuild.\n\n**Edges** are typed and evidence-bearing: `{src, dst, kind, basis, confidence}`\nwhere `basis` points at the events / lines / commits that justify the edge.\nCandidate kinds, across artifact types:\n\n- `continuation`, `supersession` — between conversations, from turnbridge/ledger\n  `links`; explicit, high confidence.\n- `imports`, `documents`, `discusses`, `implements` — cross-type edges\n  (file→file, doc→file, conversation→file, commit→decision).\n- `shared_file`, `same_author`, `temporal_adjacency` — weak co-occurrence\n  edges, kept clearly lower-confidence so they are not mistaken for explicit\n  references.\n\nExplicit edges are authoritative; derived ones always carry their basis and a\nconfidence so a consumer can filter.\n\n## Inputs (by build stage)\n\n1. **Conversations (first).** Read Conversation Ledger via its library API\n   (`readEvents`, `sortEvents`) or `cledger export`, grouped by\n   `conversation.id`. Everything for the first edges is already present:\n   `links` (turnbridge `continuation`), `actor.id` (author), `context.repo/\n   branch/head/cwd` (grounding + the join key to code nodes), `occurred_at`\n   (temporal), and file paths inside tool-call content blocks (`Read`/`Edit`/\n   `Bash`) — which become conversation→file edges the moment file nodes exist.\n2. **Code and docs (next).** Walk the working tree: import/require graphs for\n   code, link/reference graphs for docs. These are the extractors that turn the\n   conversation subgraph into a repo-wide graph.\n3. **Commits/decisions (later).** git history joins commits to the\n   conversations and decisions that produced them.\n\n## Downstream target: tagseq2tagseq\n\ntagseq2tagseq trains on a **text-attributed document graph**, and its extractor\nlayer is exactly the abstraction this tool needs: three protocols —\n`ContentSource` (iterate `Document{identifier, normalized_identifier, content,\nmetadata}`), `LinkExtractor` (raw link targets from content), `LinkNormalizer`\n(canonical ids) — feeding a `GraphBuilder` that writes `graph.jsonl`, one node\nper line with `outgoing`/`incoming` edge sets, `char_count`, and `metadata`,\nwhich the model then traverses (e.g. random walk).\n\nContext Graph should emit exactly that shape so it drops in as new\n`source_type`s:\n\n- a conversation node → a `Document` whose `content` is its rendered transcript\n  (reuse turnbridge's literal-transcript renderer), `normalized_identifier` =\n  ledger `conversation.id`; a file node → a `Document` whose content is the file;\n- typed edges collapse into `outgoing`/`incoming` for training, but the richer\n  `{kind, basis, confidence}` is retained in `metadata` so nothing is lost;\n- alternatively, implement each extractor *as* a tagseq2tagseq\n  `ContentSource`+`LinkExtractor` pair rather than re-emitting JSONL. Which side\n  owns the adapter is open.\n\nBecause tagseq2tagseq already calls its input a text-attributed graph, the two\ntools share a vocabulary: Context Graph is the builder, tagseq2tagseq is one\ntraverser.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **Language.** Reads TS (conversation-ledger) and feeds Python\n  (tagseq2tagseq). Emit neutral `graph.jsonl` from TS, or implement as Python\n  tagseq2tagseq extractors, or split. Undecided.\n- **Storage.** Recompute on demand (pure index) vs. persist (and if persisted,\n  in git notes like the ledger, or a separate artifact).\n- **Cross-repo / cross-user.** The ledger can hold synced conversations from\n  collaborators; does the graph span repos, and how are identifiers namespaced.\n- **Edge derivation ownership.** Which derived edges belong here vs. are left to\n  intent-recall to assert with its confidence machinery, to avoid two tools\n  claiming the same relationship differently.\n- **Rebuild determinism.** Guaranteeing the same inputs produce byte-identical\n  `graph.jsonl` (stable node/edge ordering) so the graph is diffable and\n  cacheable.\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/context-graph/docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md","originalFile":"# WIP technical design\n\n**Status:** exploratory impressions, nothing locked in — including the\nimplementation language. This records how the problem looks after building\nconversation-ledger and turnbridge, so later work does not start cold.\n\n## Inputs\n\nThe only required input is Conversation Ledger. Events are read through its\nlibrary API (`readEvents`, `sortEvents`) or `cledger export`, grouped by\n`conversation.id`. Everything needed to derive first-class edges is already\npresent:\n\n- `conversation.id` / `seq` — node identity and intra-conversation order.\n- `links` (e.g. `{rel:\"continues\", target}`) — turnbridge's `continuation`\n  lineage edges, and any future `supersession` links.\n- `actor.id` — author, for same-author edges.\n- `context.repo` / `branch` / `head` / `cwd` — repository grounding and, later,\n  the join key to code/doc nodes.\n- `occurred_at` — temporal adjacency.\n- content blocks — file paths mentioned in tool calls (`Read`, `Edit`, `Bash`),\n  which yield conversation→file edges once file nodes exist.\n\n## Node and edge model (draft)\n\n**Nodes** are conversations to start with: `{id, repo, author, first/last\nactivity, turn_count, title}`, referencing the ledger rather than copying\ncontent. The model is deliberately widened from day one so conversations are a\n*subgraph* of an eventual repo-wide graph — a node carries a `type`\n(`conversation` now; `file`, `commit`, `decision` later) and a stable\n`normalized_identifier`.\n\n**Edges** are typed and evidence-bearing: `{src, dst, kind, basis:[event_ids],\nconfidence}`. Candidate kinds:\n\n- `continuation` — from turnbridge `links`; explicit, high confidence.\n- `supersession` — a later decision overriding an earlier one (explicit when\n  linked; otherwise a candidate for intent-recall, not this tool, to assert).\n- `shared_file` — two conversations touched the same path.\n- `same_author` / `temporal_adjacency` — weak, co-occurrence edges; kept\n  clearly lower-confidence so they are not mistaken for explicit links.\n- `reference` — a conversation names another conversation/commit/file.\n\nExplicit edges (from `links`) are authoritative; derived edges (co-occurrence)\nalways carry their basis and a confidence so a consumer can filter.\n\n## Downstream target: tagseq2tagseq\n\ntagseq2tagseq trains on a **text-attributed document graph**. Its extractor\nlayer is three protocols — `ContentSource` (iterate `Document{identifier,\nnormalized_identifier, content, metadata}`), `LinkExtractor` (raw link targets\nfrom content), `LinkNormalizer` (canonical ids) — feeding a `GraphBuilder` that\nwrites `graph.jsonl`, one node per line with `outgoing`/`incoming` edge sets,\n`char_count`, and `metadata`, which the model then traverses (e.g. random\nwalk).\n\nThis tool should emit exactly that shape, so the conversation graph drops into\ntagseq2tagseq as a new `source_type`:\n\n- a conversation node → a `Document` whose `content` is its rendered transcript\n  (reuse turnbridge's literal-transcript renderer) and whose `normalized_id`\n  is the ledger `conversation.id`;\n- typed edges collapse into `outgoing`/`incoming` for training, but the richer\n  `{kind, basis, confidence}` is retained in `metadata` so nothing is lost;\n- alternatively, implement the conversation graph *as* a tagseq2tagseq\n  `ContentSource`+`LinkExtractor` pair rather than re-emitting JSONL. Which side\n  owns the adapter is open.\n\n## Generalization to a whole-repo graph\n\nThe larger ambition — graph every cross-file connection (code imports, doc\nlinks, conversation↔file, commit↔decision) — is the same `GraphBuilder` with\nmore extractors: one `ContentSource`/`LinkExtractor` per content type, sharing\none identifier namespace so a conversation node and the file it discusses are\njoinable. The conversation extractor is just the first. Keeping node identity\nand edge shape uniform now is what lets that compose later instead of forcing a\nrebuild. Whether the repo-wide graph lives here or in a later sibling is\nundecided; the design assumes it should be able to.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **Language.** Reads TS (conversation-ledger) and feeds Python\n  (tagseq2tagseq). Emit neutral `graph.jsonl` from TS, or implement as a Python\n  tagseq2tagseq extractor, or split. Undecided.\n- **Storage.** Recompute the graph on demand (pure index) vs. persist it (and\n  if persisted, in git notes like the ledger, or a separate artifact).\n- **Cross-repo / cross-user.** The ledger can hold synced conversations from\n  collaborators; does the graph span repos, and how are identifiers namespaced\n  across them.\n- **Edge derivation ownership.** Which derived edges belong here vs. are left to\n  intent-recall to assert with its confidence machinery, to avoid two tools\n  claiming the same relationship differently.\n- **Rebuild determinism.** Guaranteeing the same events produce byte-identical\n  `graph.jsonl` (stable node/edge ordering) so the graph is diffable and\n  cacheable.\n","structuredPatch":[{"lines":[" implementation language. This records how the problem looks after building"," conversation-ledger and turnbridge, so later work does not start cold."," ","-## Inputs","+## Node and edge model (draft)"," ","-The only required input is Conversation Ledger. Events are read through its","-library API (`readEvents`, `sortEvents`) or `cledger export`, grouped by","-`conversation.id`. Everything needed to derive first-class edges is already","-present:","+The graph is **heterogeneous from the start**. A node carries a `type`","+(`conversation` is implemented first; `file`, `commit`, `decision` follow), a","+stable `normalized_identifier` in 